  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 257 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday night...\n\nFairly benign weather with mostly clear skies is expected over the \nnext few days as surface high pressure centered over the Ohio Valley \nmoves eastward towards the mid-Atlantic. At the mid-upper levels, \nridging will build east from the Plains while a strong upper trough \ndevelops into the intermountain west. Temperatures today were on \naverage 2-3 degrees warmer than guidance. RRFS forecast soundings \nverified best today and have the warmest readings for Friday and \nSaturday along with the NBM 90th percentile. Therefore, have raised \nhighs to around 70 for Friday and in the lower 70s for Saturday. For \nreference, the record high temperature for Saturday is 73 set in \n1990. Long nights and clear skies along with a dry airmass will \ncontinue to lead to large diurnal temperature swings (30-35 degrees) \nespecially in more rural areas. \n\nSunday through Sunday night...\n\nA secondary shortwave trough presently over the WA coast will rotate \naround the parent upper trough and shift eastward into the Plains \nlate Saturday/Saturday night. This system will move into the MS \nvalley and central Indiana on Sunday as it is expected to phase with \na northern stream shortwave trough moving into the western Great \nLakes. Meanwhile an upper trough/closed low off the Carolina coast \nwill shift northward. On the northern periphery of this system a \nreinforcement to a ridge of surface high pressure extending back \ninto the the Ohio Valley. As a result, limited moisture return \nalong weak low level flow is expected. The result of this weak \nlow level response will be limited moisture return ahead of the \ncold front, with meager MU/SBCAPE less than 100 J/KG noted in a \nshallow 2-3 km AGL layer precluding any TS potential. The \nnoteworthy 1-1.25 PWAT on forecast soundings, ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance will be realized with an anafrontal type \nsetup where forcing is strongest in the \"colder\" near post surface\nfront. NBM PoPs were running 70-90%, which seems a little high \nconsidering the overall weak forcing expected, however the \nstratiform nature of the precip will likely make rainfall fairly \nwidespread and light.\n\nMonday through Tuesday... \n\nIn the wake of the shortwave trough and cold frontal passage Sunday \nnight, surface high pressure will build southward into the upper MS \nvalley. BL flow on Monday will be N-NW which should keep skies \nmostly cloudy owing to lake Michigan influences with strato-cu \ndevelopment in the post frontal airmass. Additional shortwave energy \nejecting out of the longwave trough over the eastern PAC/Gulf of \nAlaska will develop into the Plains Tuesday, while surface/upper \nridging build into the MS valley leading to clearing skies, albeit \nmuch colder than recently with temps in the lower-mid 40s (about 5 \ndegrees below normal). \n\nWednesday and Thanksgiving...\n\nThe 00Z run of the deterministic Euro which was highly aggressive and \na major outlier compared to all other medium range guidance \nunfortunately tainted the NBM for this forecast package. As a \nresult, NBM is carrying low PoPs (because of limited members \ncontributing) along with a rain/snow mix (because of temps near \nfreezing late at night/early mornings) which will show up in the \npublic forecast output. It is highly unlikely (< 5 percent chance)\nthat the 00Z Euro run would verify given the persistent signal \nfor the ENSO enhanced southern stream/Gulf Coast region for \nfavorable areas of cyclogenesis. These recent seasonal trends \ncoupled with a relatively weak/progressive northern stream over \nthe U.S/Canadian border and limited arctic air masses/snowpack \nwill fail to augment any stronger CAA/frontogenetic response at or\nnear the latitude of central Indiana.",
